soc: sunxi: cpu_comm: the software spinlocks as the vendor and the firmware run them

The twelve 12-byte lock entries in shared memory are one protocol on both
processors (the vendor's tridspinlock.c in the ARM driver and in
display.bin): byte 0 says who is in the critical section, byte 1 who holds
the lock. Our port wrote byte 3 as "in the critical section", did not wait
for the other side there, and never wrote byte 1, so a lock the ARM held
looked free to the MIPS and both changed ref_count and thread at once. On a
warm start, when Linux registers its routines while the freshly started
MIPS copies its routine table under lock 2, that ended as
"SpinUnLock(2): no thread_id", lock 2 at ref 1 for ever, every open of the
HDMI receiver refused and the wall dark (TODO 2.63, doku/202, doku/203).

enterCritical() now waits for byte 0, takes hardware lock id+2, looks
again and writes the ARM; spinLock() takes byte 1 and waits while the
other side holds it; comm_SpinUnLock() leaves a lock the MIPS holds alone.
The analysis and the patch come from an offline package
(arbeit/mips-sperre-20261012): the firmware and the vendor driver read
back, a model of all interleavings, a counter-side test (old: 6 firmware
asserts in 30 s, new: 0 in 60 s). On top: a load that adopts the live
region releases whatever an earlier Linux left as held by the ARM - after
a reset in the middle of a standby the routine lock read "cpu 0, ref 1,
thread 0xff". Measured on the HY300PRO-G-EU: 10 warm starts clean with the
port; before about 2 of 11 dark.
